#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151890 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3abe724a-6aec-5c11-8a00-d92e616a06d7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with a Repeated Root"}] :question/id #uuid "10aa6257-c3f8-59c6-802e-b4a29fca49ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-6627" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$2x^2 y'' + 6 xy' + 2y = 0, \\qquad x>0$." :question/worked-solution "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + 6x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} + 2x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\n2λ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + 6λx^{λ} + 2x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[2λ(λ - 1) + 6λ + 2] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2λ(λ - 1) + 6λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2λ^{2} + 4λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n2(λ + 1)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation has a double root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= c_{1}x^{-1} + c_{2}x^{-1}\\ln x \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{x} + \\frac{c_{2}\\ln x}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151891 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f9ca4b7-157d-56c9-9302-e7ad08c615c0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with a Repeated Root"}] :question/id #uuid "ff0b0603-e0bf-526a-9b4b-d2a4886ba2c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6628" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y =c_1 x^{10}+ c_2x ^{10}\\ln x$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0,\\quad y'(1)=1, \\quad x>0$." :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0$. Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0= c_1$.\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 1$. Differentiating $y$, we get\n$y^{′}|= 10c_{1}x^{9} + c_{2}x^{9} + 10c_{2}x^{9}\\ln x$.\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=1$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 10c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\\n1 &= 0 + c_{2} \\\\\nc_{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = x ^{10}\\ln x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3abe724a-6aec-5c11-8a00-d92e616a06d7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with a Repeated Root"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "834070a6-752a-509b-b1ff-2bac02438364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x^{-1} + c_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150085} {:answer/id #uuid "85319001-94fc-5a32-ace4-8f6ca3b00959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150086} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff66833-17bc-51c9-861a-bbbdadc44dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x^{-2} + c_{2}x^{-2}\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150087} {:answer/id #uuid "18576fab-5f8e-5553-be21-cb8d2d87f47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150088} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0d7089-bf13-5625-a91f-594f02fa590c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a0d7089-bf13-5625-a91f-594f02fa590c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150089} :answer-field/id #uuid "95c6adf7-